  <h2>3. Perfect Reasoners, Proof-Carrying Outputs &amp; Lawful Composition as Lifeblood</h2>

  <p>What is real Artificial Intelligence? Not a stochastic autocomplete engine emitting unverified prose that sounds plausible. A real artificial intelligence is a <strong>perfect reasoner and inferer</strong> whose every output is paired with its own constructive <strong>proof of correctness and rationale</strong> as a single inseparable dependent pair:</p>

  <div class="eq-box">
    $$\mathrm{Reasoner}(x : A) \;:\; \sum_{y : B} \mathrm{Proof}(x,\, y)$$
  </div>

  <p>In Hyperactivity, the proof is never an afterthought or a post-hoc "chain-of-thought" explanation that can contradict the answer. By the Curry–Howard–Voevodsky correspondence, the proof <em>is</em> the executable program, verified in normal form by the self-hosted cubical kernel (<code>lib/check.hypercubical</code>).</p>

  <h3>Lawful Composition as the Lifeblood of Intelligence</h3>

  <p>Why do current neural agents collapse on long-horizon engineering, mathematics, and science? Because errors in untyped statistical pipelines compound exponentially. If a single heuristic step succeeds with probability $1 - \epsilon$, chaining $k$ steps degrades as:</p>

  <div class="eq-box">
    $$P_{\text{statistical}}(\text{correct after } k \text{ steps}) \;=\; (1 - \epsilon)^k \;\xrightarrow{\;k \to \infty\;}\; 0$$
  </div>

  <p><strong>Lawful composition</strong> in a proof-theoretic knowledge graph eliminates compounding error structurally. Every derivation step is a typed cubical morphism $(f, \pi_f)$ whose codomain matches the exact domain and invariant preconditions of the next morphism $(g, \pi_g)$. Composing them produces $(g \circ f,\; \pi_g \circ \pi_f)$ with <strong>zero error accumulation across arbitrary depth</strong>. If step $999{,}999$ violates an invariant, the cubical boundary fails to close and the kernel rejects it deterministically before it can ever corrupt downstream knowledge.</p>

  <h3>Compression = Intelligence via the Semantics of Interaction</h3>

  <p>The deepest unification in the theory of intelligence (Shannon, 1948; Kolmogorov, 1965; Solomonoff, 1964; Hutter, 2005) is that <strong>compression and intelligence are the same mathematical object</strong>. Predicting the next event of a universe with minimal surprise is strictly identical to encoding its ordered record $R_{&lt;t}$ in minimal bits: every bit saved is an exact structural regularity discovered, and every bit remaining is irreducible ignorance. Yet classical formulations leave a fatal chasm:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Static Shannon coding</strong> assumes a fixed, externally given probability distribution $p(x)$ — it cannot explain how an agent <em>discovers</em> structure from interaction.</li>
    <li><strong>Kolmogorov / Solomonoff / AIXI</strong> defines universal compression $K(x) = \min \{|p| : U(p) = x\}$ and universal prior $M(x) = \sum_{U(p)=x\ast} 2^{-|p|}$, but is <strong>uncomputable</strong> because it demands exhaustive search over all halting Turing machines.</li>
    <li><strong>Neural compressors</strong> approximate $p_\theta(x_t \mid x_{&lt;t})$ with billions of opaque floating-point weights, carrying no round-trip proof and breaking down whenever the world shifts outside the training distribution.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Hyperactivity bridges this chasm by deriving <strong>information dynamics directly from the semantics of interaction</strong> (<code>lib/compress.hypercubical</code>, <code>lib/compress-stream.hypercubical</code>, <code>lib/optimality.hypercubical</code>). Instead of searching over Turing machines or fitting static weights, every interaction step between observer and ordered record $R_{&lt;t}$ deterministically updates the nested tower of <strong>causal context fibers</strong> $\mathrm{Fib}_k(c_t)$ across depths $k \in \{4, 3, 2, 1, 0\}$ in $O(1)$ per symbol — with <strong>zero search, zero serialized lookup tables, zero floating-point arithmetic, and zero probability axioms</strong>.</p>

  <div class="eq-box">
    $$x' \;=\; \left\lfloor \frac{x}{f(s)} \right\rfloor M \;+\; C(s) \;+\; \big(x \bmod f(s)\big), \qquad \Delta \log_2 x \;=\; \log_2 \frac{M}{f(s)} + O\!\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)$$
  </div>

  <p>Here $f(s)$ is the exact integer frequency of symbol $s$ inside the active context fiber, $C(s) = \sum_{a &lt; s} f(a)$ is its cumulative discrete measure, and $M = \sum_a f(a)$ is the total fiber measure. Folded into streaming $O(N)$ renormalized <strong>rANS</strong> (Duda, 2009), this transition is proved bijective purely from exact bignum division laws (<code>wdiv-mod</code>, <code>wdiv-unique</code>, <code>wmod-unique</code>), yielding universal <code>refl</code> round-trip decoding (<code>decode (encode x) ≡ x</code>) alongside <strong>Kraft's inequality</strong> ($\sum_i 2^{-\ell_i} \le 1$), the exact rANS Shannon-rate upper bound, and <strong>pigeonhole incompressibility</strong> (<code>lib/optimality.hypercubical</code>).</p>

  <p>Five exact cubical equivalence mechanisms — every one a two-sided <code>Equiv</code> transportable via univalence (<code>ua</code>) — compose lawfully to crystallize structure at every level:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Causal Context-Fiber Exclusion &amp; Telescoping (<code>compress-record</code>, <code>compress-given</code>):</strong> If $x_t$ has not yet occurred in depth-$k$ fiber $\mathrm{Fib}_k(c_t)$, the encoder emits an escape and drops to depth $k-1$ while <em>strictly excluding</em> every symbol already ruled out at depth $k$ (exact fiber exclusion down to earliest-content ground coding). Feeding concatenated records satisfies exact <strong>compositional telescoping</strong> (<code>cstate-feed-append</code>): $\mathrm{feed}(S, x \mathbin{\Vert} y) \equiv \mathrm{feed}(\mathrm{feed}(S, x), y)$.</li>
    <li><strong>Bidirectional Context-Class Quotienting (<code>compress-record-upto</code>):</strong> Factors any symbol $x$ along an equivalence relation into its class and intra-class fiber rank $(q(x), r(x)) \in Q \times \mathrm{Fib}_q(q(x))$, extracting structural invariants directly (e.g., <code>digit-class</code>).</li>
